(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("llm",{"llm/what-an-llm-is-and-how-it-predicts-tokens":{"html":"\u003cp>A large language model, or LLM, is a program trained on a very large amount of text to predict what comes next. That one skill, repeated thousands of times, produces answers, code, summaries and conversation. Engineers who understand the prediction loop make better decisions about prompts, costs and safety than those who treat the model as a mind or as a search engine.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The prediction loop: text in, one token out, repeat\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why the same prompt can give different answers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What models are good at, and where they fail\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A mental model you can use when something goes wrong\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-loop\">The loop\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The model receives text, converted to numbers, and produces a score for every possible next piece of text. One piece is chosen, added to the input, and the process repeats until the model produces a stop signal or hits a length limit. Nothing else happens inside: no lookup in a database and no running of code unless you give it tools. A reply of 300 words is hundreds of small predictions in a row, each depending on everything before it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-it-feels-smart\">Why it feels smart\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Predicting text well requires capturing grammar, facts, style, reasoning patterns and code structure that appear in the training text. So the model can explain a stack trace, translate a paragraph or write a Terraform module, because those patterns were common in what it learned. It does not &quot;look up&quot; an answer. It produces the most plausible continuation, shaped by training to be helpful and honest.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"strengths-and-weak-spots\">Strengths and weak spots\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Strong\u003c/strong>: summarising, rewriting, classifying, extracting fields from messy text, drafting code and explanations, translating between formats.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Weaker\u003c/strong>: exact arithmetic on long numbers, counting characters, facts after its training cutoff, rarely seen details (an obscure flag of a tool), and anything that needs real-time data.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Risky\u003c/strong>: confident but wrong answers, called hallucinations. A fluent sentence is not evidence of a true one.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>The practical rule: use the model for language and pattern work, and use tools (a calculator, a search, a database) for exact facts.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-mental-model-that-helps-debugging\">A mental model that helps debugging\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When a result is wrong, ask three questions. \u003cstrong>What did the model actually see?\u003c/strong> Print the exact input, including the system prompt and any retrieved text. \u003cstrong>Is the answer in the input?\u003c/strong> If not, the model must guess. \u003cstrong>Is the task a language task?\u003c/strong> If it needs exactness, add a tool or a check. Most &quot;model problems&quot; are input problems. Keep the model&#39;s output as untrusted data: validate it before it reaches a database, a shell or a customer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You ask a model for the exact SHA-256 of a string and it answers with a plausible 64-character hash. Why is this unreliable?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The model predicts plausible text; it does not run the hash algorithm. Use a tool or code for exact computation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Language task versus exact task.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-confident-wrong-flag\">Incident: The confident wrong flag\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An on-call engineer pasted a model answer into a runbook without checking it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl rollout restart deploy/web --graceful-period=30\nkubectl rollout restart --help | grep -i grace\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout restart deploy/web --graceful-period=30\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error: unknown flag: --graceful-period\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout restart --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">help\u003c/span> | grep -i grace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(no output)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The model had produced a flag that looks right but does not exist for that command. The runbook was corrected using the tool&#39;s own help, and the team added a rule: commands from a model are tested in a safe environment and checked against the official reference before they enter documentation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating fluent output as verified output\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Asking the model for exact arithmetic or recent facts without tools\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not looking at the exact input the model received\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting model output straight into a shell or database\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-loop","text":"The loop"},{"id":"why-it-feels-smart","text":"Why it feels smart"},{"id":"strengths-and-weak-spots","text":"Strengths and weak spots"},{"id":"a-mental-model-that-helps-debugging","text":"A mental model that helps debugging"},{"id":"incident-the-confident-wrong-flag","text":"Incident: The confident wrong flag"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Models overview","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/about-claude/models/overview"},{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Glossary","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/resources/glossary"}],"text":"A large language model, or LLM, is a program trained on a very large amount of text to predict what comes next. That one skill, repeated thousands of times, produces answers, code, summaries and conversation. Engineers who understand the prediction loop make better decisions about prompts, costs and safety than those who treat the model as a mind or as a search engine. What you will learn The prediction loop: text in, one token out, repeat Why the same prompt can give different answers What models are good at, and where they fail A mental model you can use when something goes wrong The loop The model receives text, converted to numbers, and produces a score for every possible next piece of text. One piece is chosen, added to the input, and the process repeats until the model produces a stop signal or hits a length limit. Nothing else happens inside: no lookup in a database and no running of code unless you give it tools. A reply of 300 words is hundreds of small predictions in a row, each depending on everything before it. Why it feels smart Predicting text well requires capturing grammar, facts, style, reasoning patterns and code structure that appear in the training text. So the model can explain a stack trace, translate a paragraph or write a Terraform module, because those patterns were common in what it learned. It does not \"look up\" an answer. It produces the most plausible continuation, shaped by training to be helpful and honest. Strengths and weak spots Strong : summarising, rewriting, classifying, extracting fields from messy text, drafting code and explanations, translating between formats. Weaker : exact arithmetic on long numbers, counting characters, facts after its training cutoff, rarely seen details (an obscure flag of a tool), and anything that needs real-time data. Risky : confident but wrong answers, called hallucinations. A fluent sentence is not evidence of a true one. The practical rule: use the model for language and pattern work, and use tools (a calculator, a search, a database) for exact facts. A mental model that helps debugging When a result is wrong, ask three questions. What did the model actually see? Print the exact input, including the system prompt and any retrieved text. Is the answer in the input? If not, the model must guess. Is the task a language task? If it needs exactness, add a tool or a check. Most \"model problems\" are input problems. Keep the model&#39;s output as untrusted data: validate it before it reaches a database, a shell or a customer. Predict first. You ask a model for the exact SHA-256 of a string and it answers with a plausible 64-character hash. Why is this unreliable? Your guess Answer: The model predicts plausible text; it does not run the hash algorithm. Use a tool or code for exact computation. Language task versus exact task. Incident: The confident wrong flag Incident An on-call engineer pasted a model answer into a runbook without checking it. $ kubectl rollout restart deploy/web --graceful-period=30 error: unknown flag: --graceful-period $ kubectl rollout restart -- help | grep -i grace (no output) The model had produced a flag that looks right but does not exist for that command. The runbook was corrected using the tool&#39;s own help, and the team added a rule: commands from a model are tested in a safe environment and checked against the official reference before they enter documentation. Common mistakes Treating fluent output as verified output Asking the model for exact arithmetic or recent facts without tools Not looking at the exact input the model received Putting model output straight into a shell or database"},"llm/tokens-context-windows-and-limits":{"html":"\u003cp>Models do not read characters or words. They read tokens, small pieces of text, and every limit and every price is counted in tokens. Knowing what a token is and what fills the context window explains why long chats get worse, why prices depend on length, and why a prompt that works on a short example can fail on real data.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What a token is, and rough sizes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What counts toward the context window\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Input limits versus output limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Strategies for text that does not fit\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tokens\">Tokens\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A tokenizer splits text into pieces such as whole common words, parts of rare words, punctuation and spaces. As a rule of thumb, one token is about four characters of English text, or three quarters of a word. Code, other languages and unusual strings often need more tokens per character. Different model families use different tokenizers, so counts differ. Use the provider&#39;s token counting tool rather than guessing when limits matter.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-context-window\">The context window\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The context window is the total number of tokens the model can consider in one request: the system prompt, the conversation so far, any documents or tool results you include, and the answer being generated. When the sum is too large, the request fails or older text must be dropped. Long contexts also cost more and can reduce accuracy for details buried in the middle, so more text is not always better.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"input-and-output-limits\">Input and output limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Providers set a maximum number of output tokens per reply, separate from the context size. If you ask for a long report with a small output limit, the answer is cut off, and the response says it stopped because of length. Always check the stop reason in the response. A truncated JSON document is invalid JSON, which breaks the program that parses it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"response = client.messages.create(\n    model=MODEL,\n    max_tokens=1024,\n    messages=[{&quot;role&quot;: &quot;user&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: prompt}],\n)\nif response.stop_reason == &quot;max_tokens&quot;:\n    raise RuntimeError(&quot;answer was cut off; raise max_tokens or shorten the task&quot;)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">response = client.messages.create(\n    model=MODEL,\n    max_tokens=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1024\u003c/span>,\n    messages=[{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;role&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;content&quot;\u003c/span>: prompt}],\n)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> response.stop_reason == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;max_tokens&quot;\u003c/span>:\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">raise\u003c/span> RuntimeError(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;answer was cut off; raise max_tokens or shorten the task&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when-text-does-not-fit\">When text does not fit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Select\u003c/strong>: send only relevant parts, found by search or by simple rules.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Summarise\u003c/strong>: compress old conversation turns into a short summary.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Chunk\u003c/strong>: process a long document in pieces and combine the results.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Cache\u003c/strong>: reuse a large, unchanging prefix, such as instructions or a reference document, using prompt caching to cut cost and delay.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Measure\u003c/strong>: log input and output token counts for each request, so cost and growth are visible.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A chat application sends the full history every turn. After 40 turns the answers get slower and costlier. Why, and what do you change?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Every turn resends all earlier tokens, so input grows each time. Summarise or trim old turns and cache stable parts.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Input tokens add up.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-report-that-stopped-in-the-middle\">Incident: The report that stopped in the middle\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A nightly job that asked a model for a JSON report failed to parse its output.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python summarize.py\npython -c &quot;print(resp.stop_reason, resp.usage.output_tokens)&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python summarize.py\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">json.decoder.JSONDecodeError: Unterminated string starting at: line 41 column 12\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;print(resp.stop_reason, resp.usage.output_tokens)&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">max_tokens 1024\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The model hit the output limit in the middle of the JSON. The fix raised max_tokens, asked for shorter field values, and added a check that raises an error when the stop reason is max_tokens instead of passing broken text to the parser.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Guessing token counts instead of measuring\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring the stop reason\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Resending unlimited history\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting entire documents in when a few paragraphs would do\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"tokens","text":"Tokens"},{"id":"the-context-window","text":"The context window"},{"id":"input-and-output-limits","text":"Input and output limits"},{"id":"when-text-does-not-fit","text":"When text does not fit"},{"id":"incident-the-report-that-stopped-in-the-middle","text":"Incident: The report that stopped in the middle"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Context windows","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/context-windows"},{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Token counting","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/token-counting"}],"text":"Models do not read characters or words. They read tokens, small pieces of text, and every limit and every price is counted in tokens. Knowing what a token is and what fills the context window explains why long chats get worse, why prices depend on length, and why a prompt that works on a short example can fail on real data. What you will learn What a token is, and rough sizes What counts toward the context window Input limits versus output limits Strategies for text that does not fit Tokens A tokenizer splits text into pieces such as whole common words, parts of rare words, punctuation and spaces. As a rule of thumb, one token is about four characters of English text, or three quarters of a word. Code, other languages and unusual strings often need more tokens per character. Different model families use different tokenizers, so counts differ. Use the provider&#39;s token counting tool rather than guessing when limits matter. The context window The context window is the total number of tokens the model can consider in one request: the system prompt, the conversation so far, any documents or tool results you include, and the answer being generated. When the sum is too large, the request fails or older text must be dropped. Long contexts also cost more and can reduce accuracy for details buried in the middle, so more text is not always better. Input and output limits Providers set a maximum number of output tokens per reply, separate from the context size. If you ask for a long report with a small output limit, the answer is cut off, and the response says it stopped because of length. Always check the stop reason in the response. A truncated JSON document is invalid JSON, which breaks the program that parses it. response = client.messages.create( model=MODEL, max_tokens= 1024 , messages=[{ \"role\" : \"user\" , \"content\" : prompt}], ) if response.stop_reason == \"max_tokens\" : raise RuntimeError( \"answer was cut off; raise max_tokens or shorten the task\" ) When text does not fit Select : send only relevant parts, found by search or by simple rules. Summarise : compress old conversation turns into a short summary. Chunk : process a long document in pieces and combine the results. Cache : reuse a large, unchanging prefix, such as instructions or a reference document, using prompt caching to cut cost and delay. Measure : log input and output token counts for each request, so cost and growth are visible. Predict first. A chat application sends the full history every turn. After 40 turns the answers get slower and costlier. Why, and what do you change? Your guess Answer: Every turn resends all earlier tokens, so input grows each time. Summarise or trim old turns and cache stable parts. Input tokens add up. Incident: The report that stopped in the middle Incident A nightly job that asked a model for a JSON report failed to parse its output. $ python summarize.py json.decoder.JSONDecodeError: Unterminated string starting at: line 41 column 12 $ python -c \"print(resp.stop_reason, resp.usage.output_tokens)\" max_tokens 1024 The model hit the output limit in the middle of the JSON. The fix raised max_tokens, asked for shorter field values, and added a check that raises an error when the stop reason is max_tokens instead of passing broken text to the parser. Common mistakes Guessing token counts instead of measuring Ignoring the stop reason Resending unlimited history Putting entire documents in when a few paragraphs would do"},"llm/sampling-temperature-and-determinism":{"html":"\u003cp>After the model scores every possible next token, something must choose one. That choice is called sampling, and it explains why answers vary between runs. Getting predictable behaviour for a pipeline is partly a matter of settings, and mostly a matter of designing the system so that small variation does not matter.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How sampling chooses a token\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What temperature and top-p do\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What you can and cannot make deterministic\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Designing around variation\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"probabilities\">Probabilities\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For each step the model produces a probability for every token. If &quot;Paris&quot; has 92 percent and &quot;Lyon&quot; has 3 percent after &quot;The capital of France is&quot;, then most runs choose Paris. Different prompts and different points in a sentence have very different spreads: a factual answer is peaked, a story opening is wide.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"temperature-and-top-p\">Temperature and top-p\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Temperature\u003c/strong> rescales probabilities before choosing. Low values (near 0) make the highest-probability token win almost always, giving focused and repeatable output. Higher values flatten the choice and give more varied, creative output. \u003cstrong>Top-p\u003c/strong> limits the choice to the smallest set of tokens whose probabilities add up to p. Change one of them, not both, unless you have a reason. For extraction, classification and code, use low temperature; for brainstorming, use higher.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"response = client.messages.create(\n    model=MODEL,\n    max_tokens=300,\n    temperature=0.0,\n    messages=[{&quot;role&quot;: &quot;user&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: &quot;Classify this ticket: &quot; + ticket}],\n)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">response = client.messages.create(\n    model=MODEL,\n    max_tokens=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">300\u003c/span>,\n    temperature=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.0\u003c/span>,\n    messages=[{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;role&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;content&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Classify this ticket: &quot;\u003c/span> + ticket}],\n)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"determinism-has-limits\">Determinism has limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Even at temperature 0, results can differ slightly between calls, because of how requests are batched and how hardware does arithmetic, and any change to the model version changes outputs. Some newer models and modes fix or ignore sampling settings, so check the documentation for the model you use. Never write tests that require an exact sentence from a model, unless you have pinned the model and accept occasional breaks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"designing-around-variation\">Designing around variation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Ask for \u003cstrong>structured output\u003c/strong> and validate it; the format stays stable even when wording varies.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Test \u003cstrong>properties\u003c/strong>, not strings: the answer must contain a valid category, a number in range, or cite a given document.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Run an important test several times and look at the \u003cstrong>pass rate\u003c/strong>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>For decisions, use a constrained set of answers and a fallback to a human when the model is unsure.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pin a \u003cstrong>model version\u003c/strong> in production and re-run your tests before upgrading.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A unit test compares a model reply to an exact sentence and fails once a week. What should it check instead?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> A property of the answer, such as valid JSON with an allowed category, run several times with a pass-rate threshold.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Test behaviour, not wording.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-flaky-classifier\">Incident: The flaky classifier\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A ticket router sent the same message to different queues on different days.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"for i in 1 2 3 4 5; do python route.py &quot;refund not received&quot;; done\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> i \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> 1 2 3 4 5; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span> python route.py \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;refund not received&quot;\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">done\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">billing\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">billing\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">payments\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">billing\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">payments\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The prompt asked for free text, the temperature was left at the default, and &quot;payments&quot; and &quot;billing&quot; both seemed reasonable. The team listed the allowed queues in the prompt, requested one value from that list in JSON, set a low temperature, and added a test that runs each sample ten times.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using high temperature for extraction or classification\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expecting exact repeatability\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tuning temperature and top-p together without measuring\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not pinning the model version\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"probabilities","text":"Probabilities"},{"id":"temperature-and-top-p","text":"Temperature and top-p"},{"id":"determinism-has-limits","text":"Determinism has limits"},{"id":"designing-around-variation","text":"Designing around variation"},{"id":"incident-the-flaky-classifier","text":"Incident: The flaky classifier"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic API reference: Messages","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/api/messages"},{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Reduce hallucinations","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-hallucinations"}],"text":"After the model scores every possible next token, something must choose one. That choice is called sampling, and it explains why answers vary between runs. Getting predictable behaviour for a pipeline is partly a matter of settings, and mostly a matter of designing the system so that small variation does not matter. What you will learn How sampling chooses a token What temperature and top-p do What you can and cannot make deterministic Designing around variation Probabilities For each step the model produces a probability for every token. If \"Paris\" has 92 percent and \"Lyon\" has 3 percent after \"The capital of France is\", then most runs choose Paris. Different prompts and different points in a sentence have very different spreads: a factual answer is peaked, a story opening is wide. Temperature and top-p Temperature rescales probabilities before choosing. Low values (near 0) make the highest-probability token win almost always, giving focused and repeatable output. Higher values flatten the choice and give more varied, creative output. Top-p limits the choice to the smallest set of tokens whose probabilities add up to p. Change one of them, not both, unless you have a reason. For extraction, classification and code, use low temperature; for brainstorming, use higher. response = client.messages.create( model=MODEL, max_tokens= 300 , temperature= 0.0 , messages=[{ \"role\" : \"user\" , \"content\" : \"Classify this ticket: \" + ticket}], ) Determinism has limits Even at temperature 0, results can differ slightly between calls, because of how requests are batched and how hardware does arithmetic, and any change to the model version changes outputs. Some newer models and modes fix or ignore sampling settings, so check the documentation for the model you use. Never write tests that require an exact sentence from a model, unless you have pinned the model and accept occasional breaks. Designing around variation Ask for structured output and validate it; the format stays stable even when wording varies. Test properties , not strings: the answer must contain a valid category, a number in range, or cite a given document. Run an important test several times and look at the pass rate . For decisions, use a constrained set of answers and a fallback to a human when the model is unsure. Pin a model version in production and re-run your tests before upgrading. Predict first. A unit test compares a model reply to an exact sentence and fails once a week. What should it check instead? Your guess Answer: A property of the answer, such as valid JSON with an allowed category, run several times with a pass-rate threshold. Test behaviour, not wording. Incident: The flaky classifier Incident A ticket router sent the same message to different queues on different days. $ for i in 1 2 3 4 5; do python route.py \"refund not received\" ; done billing billing payments billing payments The prompt asked for free text, the temperature was left at the default, and \"payments\" and \"billing\" both seemed reasonable. The team listed the allowed queues in the prompt, requested one value from that list in JSON, set a low temperature, and added a test that runs each sample ten times. Common mistakes Using high temperature for extraction or classification Expecting exact repeatability Tuning temperature and top-p together without measuring Not pinning the model version"},"llm/training-fine-tuning-and-where-knowledge-comes-from":{"html":"\u003cp>A model&#39;s knowledge comes from training, and training ended at some date. Your documents, your tickets and yesterday&#39;s news were never part of it. Understanding the stages of training, and the difference between knowledge in the model and knowledge in the prompt, tells you when to use context, retrieval, tools or training.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Pre-training and post-training in plain terms\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The knowledge cutoff and why facts go stale\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why hallucinations happen\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ways to add knowledge: context, retrieval, tools, fine-tuning\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stages-of-training\">Stages of training\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Pre-training\u003c/strong> teaches the model to predict text from a huge collection of documents and code. This gives it language, broad facts and skills. \u003cstrong>Post-training\u003c/strong> then shapes behaviour with examples and feedback so that the model follows instructions, refuses harmful requests and writes in a helpful style. Together they produce an assistant. The details differ by provider, and are not something you control when you call an API.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-cutoff\">The cutoff\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The training data ends at a date, called the knowledge cutoff. After that, the model knows nothing unless you provide it. This affects library versions, product names, prices, laws and current events. A model may also be unsure about its own cutoff, so do not ask it what is current. Put the current date and the facts you need into the prompt, or give it a search tool.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-it-makes-things-up\">Why it makes things up\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The model is trained to produce plausible text, and when the answer is not in its training or in your prompt, a plausible-sounding answer is still the most likely continuation. Rare facts, precise numbers, citations and names of obscure APIs are the most common places it invents. You reduce this by giving source text and asking for answers only from it, allowing &quot;I don&#39;t know&quot; as a valid answer, and asking for quotes that you can check.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"adding-knowledge\">Adding knowledge\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Put it in the prompt\u003c/strong> (context): best for small, current, private facts.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Retrieve it\u003c/strong>: search your documents and include the best passages (RAG).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Give tools\u003c/strong>: let the model call a database, an API or a search and use the result.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Fine-tune\u003c/strong>: train further on your examples. Useful for style, format or narrow tasks at scale; poor at adding frequently changing facts, and it costs effort to evaluate and maintain.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Start at the top of this list. Most applications never need the last one.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A support bot must answer using last week's policy changes. Which approach fits, and why not fine-tuning?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Retrieve the current policy text and put it in the prompt. Fine-tuning is slow to update and poor for facts that change often.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Facts change; keep them in context.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-library-that-did-not-exist\">Incident: The library that did not exist\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A model suggested a function that failed on first use.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python -c &quot;import boto3; boto3.client('s3').upload_stream('a', 'b')&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;import boto3; boto3.client(&#x27;s3&#x27;).upload_stream(&#x27;a&#x27;, &#x27;b&#x27;)&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">AttributeError: 'S3' object has no attribute 'upload_stream'\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The model blended real method names into one that sounded right. The developer checked the official reference and found the correct call. The team now pastes the relevant documentation page into the prompt for unfamiliar APIs and reviews generated code with tests.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Assuming the model knows recent or private facts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fine-tuning to add facts that change\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not allowing the model to say it does not know\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trusting citations without opening them\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"stages-of-training","text":"Stages of training"},{"id":"the-cutoff","text":"The cutoff"},{"id":"why-it-makes-things-up","text":"Why it makes things up"},{"id":"adding-knowledge","text":"Adding knowledge"},{"id":"incident-the-library-that-did-not-exist","text":"Incident: The library that did not exist"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Reduce hallucinations","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-hallucinations"},{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Models overview","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/about-claude/models/overview"}],"text":"A model&#39;s knowledge comes from training, and training ended at some date. Your documents, your tickets and yesterday&#39;s news were never part of it. Understanding the stages of training, and the difference between knowledge in the model and knowledge in the prompt, tells you when to use context, retrieval, tools or training. What you will learn Pre-training and post-training in plain terms The knowledge cutoff and why facts go stale Why hallucinations happen Ways to add knowledge: context, retrieval, tools, fine-tuning Stages of training Pre-training teaches the model to predict text from a huge collection of documents and code. This gives it language, broad facts and skills. Post-training then shapes behaviour with examples and feedback so that the model follows instructions, refuses harmful requests and writes in a helpful style. Together they produce an assistant. The details differ by provider, and are not something you control when you call an API. The cutoff The training data ends at a date, called the knowledge cutoff. After that, the model knows nothing unless you provide it. This affects library versions, product names, prices, laws and current events. A model may also be unsure about its own cutoff, so do not ask it what is current. Put the current date and the facts you need into the prompt, or give it a search tool. Why it makes things up The model is trained to produce plausible text, and when the answer is not in its training or in your prompt, a plausible-sounding answer is still the most likely continuation. Rare facts, precise numbers, citations and names of obscure APIs are the most common places it invents. You reduce this by giving source text and asking for answers only from it, allowing \"I don&#39;t know\" as a valid answer, and asking for quotes that you can check. Adding knowledge Put it in the prompt (context): best for small, current, private facts. Retrieve it : search your documents and include the best passages (RAG). Give tools : let the model call a database, an API or a search and use the result. Fine-tune : train further on your examples. Useful for style, format or narrow tasks at scale; poor at adding frequently changing facts, and it costs effort to evaluate and maintain. Start at the top of this list. Most applications never need the last one. Predict first. A support bot must answer using last week's policy changes. Which approach fits, and why not fine-tuning? Your guess Answer: Retrieve the current policy text and put it in the prompt. Fine-tuning is slow to update and poor for facts that change often. Facts change; keep them in context. Incident: The library that did not exist Incident A model suggested a function that failed on first use. $ python -c \"import boto3; boto3.client(&#x27;s3&#x27;).upload_stream(&#x27;a&#x27;, &#x27;b&#x27;)\" AttributeError: 'S3' object has no attribute 'upload_stream' The model blended real method names into one that sounded right. The developer checked the official reference and found the correct call. The team now pastes the relevant documentation page into the prompt for unfamiliar APIs and reviews generated code with tests. Common mistakes Assuming the model knows recent or private facts Fine-tuning to add facts that change Not allowing the model to say it does not know Trusting citations without opening them"},"llm/calling-an-llm-api":{"html":"\u003cp>Using a model from code is an ordinary HTTP API call with unusual inputs. The details that matter are the same as for any service: authentication, timeouts, retries, rate limits, logging and cost. Getting them right turns a demo into something that runs on a schedule at three in the morning.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The shape of a request: model, messages, limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Roles: system, user, assistant\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Streaming and timeouts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Errors, rate limits, retries and key handling\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-first-call\">A first call\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import os\nimport anthropic\n\nMODEL = os.environ[&quot;MODEL_ID&quot;]   # set the model name in configuration, not in code\nclient = anthropic.Anthropic()    # reads ANTHROPIC_API_KEY from the environment\n\nresponse = client.messages.create(\n    model=MODEL,\n    max_tokens=500,\n    system=&quot;You are a concise assistant for site reliability engineers.&quot;,\n    messages=[{&quot;role&quot;: &quot;user&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: &quot;Explain a 502 error in two sentences.&quot;}],\n)\nprint(response.content[0].text)\nprint(response.usage.input_tokens, response.usage.output_tokens)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> os\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> anthropic\n\nMODEL = os.environ[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;MODEL_ID&quot;\u003c/span>]   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># set the model name in configuration, not in code\u003c/span>\nclient = anthropic.Anthropic()    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># reads ANTHROPIC_API_KEY from the environment\u003c/span>\n\nresponse = client.messages.create(\n    model=MODEL,\n    max_tokens=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">500\u003c/span>,\n    system=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;You are a concise assistant for site reliability engineers.&quot;\u003c/span>,\n    messages=[{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;role&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;content&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Explain a 502 error in two sentences.&quot;\u003c/span>}],\n)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(response.content[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>].text)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(response.usage.input_tokens, response.usage.output_tokens)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A request names a model, a limit on output tokens, an optional system prompt and a list of messages. The reply has content blocks, a stop reason and token usage. Other providers have similar shapes with different field names, so wrap the call in your own small function.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"roles-and-conversation-state\">Roles and conversation state\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Messages alternate between the \u003cstrong>user\u003c/strong> and the \u003cstrong>assistant\u003c/strong>. The \u003cstrong>system\u003c/strong> prompt sets rules and context for the whole conversation. The API is stateless: it remembers nothing between calls, so your program sends the whole history each time and decides what to keep, trim or summarise. Store conversations in your own database, and treat them as personal data.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"streaming-and-timeouts\">Streaming and timeouts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Long answers take seconds. Streaming returns tokens as they are generated, so users see text immediately and your server can stop early. Always set a client timeout, and handle a stream that ends in the middle.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"with client.messages.stream(model=MODEL, max_tokens=500, messages=messages) as stream:\n    for text in stream.text_stream:\n        print(text, end=&quot;&quot;, flush=True)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">with\u003c/span> client.messages.stream(model=MODEL, max_tokens=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">500\u003c/span>, messages=messages) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">as\u003c/span> stream:\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> text \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> stream.text_stream:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(text, end=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>, flush=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"errors-limits-and-keys\">Errors, limits and keys\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Rate limits\u003c/strong> return a 429 status: slow down, and retry with exponential backoff and jitter. Respect any retry-after header.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Server errors\u003c/strong> (500, 529 overloaded) are usually temporary: retry a few times, then fail clearly.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Client errors\u003c/strong> (400, 401, 403) will not fix themselves: do not retry; log the message.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Keys\u003c/strong> belong in a secret manager or environment variable, never in code, logs, client-side JavaScript or screenshots. Use separate keys per service and per environment, set spend limits, and rotate on suspicion.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Log\u003c/strong> request IDs, model, token counts, latency and outcome, but not secrets or personal data.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Your batch job gets 429 errors during a large run. What do you add?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Backoff with jitter, a limit on concurrency, and respect for retry-after; consider batching or a queue.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Slow down instead of hammering.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-key-in-the-screenshot\">Incident: The key in the screenshot\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A teammate shared a terminal screenshot in a public channel that included an API key.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"env | grep -i anthropic\ncurl -s -o /dev/null -w &quot;%{http_code}\\n&quot; https://api.example.com/v1/models -H &quot;x-api-key: $OLD_KEY&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">env\u003c/span> | grep -i anthropic\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s -o /dev/null -w \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;%{http_code}\\n&quot;\u003c/span> https://api.example.com/v1/models -H \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;x-api-key: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$OLD_KEY\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">401\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The key was revoked as soon as the message was noticed, which is why the check now returns 401. A new key went into the secret manager, usage logs were reviewed for unexpected requests, and the team stopped printing environment variables in shared screens and added secret scanning to chat and repositories.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Hard-coding keys\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retrying client errors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No timeout on API calls\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Logging full prompts that contain personal data\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-first-call","text":"A first call"},{"id":"roles-and-conversation-state","text":"Roles and conversation state"},{"id":"streaming-and-timeouts","text":"Streaming and timeouts"},{"id":"errors-limits-and-keys","text":"Errors, limits and keys"},{"id":"incident-the-key-in-the-screenshot","text":"Incident: The key in the screenshot"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic API reference: Messages","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/api/messages"},{"t":"Anthropic API: Rate limits","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/api/rate-limits"}],"text":"Using a model from code is an ordinary HTTP API call with unusual inputs. The details that matter are the same as for any service: authentication, timeouts, retries, rate limits, logging and cost. Getting them right turns a demo into something that runs on a schedule at three in the morning. What you will learn The shape of a request: model, messages, limits Roles: system, user, assistant Streaming and timeouts Errors, rate limits, retries and key handling A first call import os import anthropic MODEL = os.environ[ \"MODEL_ID\" ] # set the model name in configuration, not in code client = anthropic.Anthropic() # reads ANTHROPIC_API_KEY from the environment response = client.messages.create( model=MODEL, max_tokens= 500 , system= \"You are a concise assistant for site reliability engineers.\" , messages=[{ \"role\" : \"user\" , \"content\" : \"Explain a 502 error in two sentences.\" }], ) print (response.content[ 0 ].text) print (response.usage.input_tokens, response.usage.output_tokens) A request names a model, a limit on output tokens, an optional system prompt and a list of messages. The reply has content blocks, a stop reason and token usage. Other providers have similar shapes with different field names, so wrap the call in your own small function. Roles and conversation state Messages alternate between the user and the assistant . The system prompt sets rules and context for the whole conversation. The API is stateless: it remembers nothing between calls, so your program sends the whole history each time and decides what to keep, trim or summarise. Store conversations in your own database, and treat them as personal data. Streaming and timeouts Long answers take seconds. Streaming returns tokens as they are generated, so users see text immediately and your server can stop early. Always set a client timeout, and handle a stream that ends in the middle. with client.messages.stream(model=MODEL, max_tokens= 500 , messages=messages) as stream: for text in stream.text_stream: print (text, end= \"\" , flush= True ) Errors, limits and keys Rate limits return a 429 status: slow down, and retry with exponential backoff and jitter. Respect any retry-after header. Server errors (500, 529 overloaded) are usually temporary: retry a few times, then fail clearly. Client errors (400, 401, 403) will not fix themselves: do not retry; log the message. Keys belong in a secret manager or environment variable, never in code, logs, client-side JavaScript or screenshots. Use separate keys per service and per environment, set spend limits, and rotate on suspicion. Log request IDs, model, token counts, latency and outcome, but not secrets or personal data. Predict first. Your batch job gets 429 errors during a large run. What do you add? Your guess Answer: Backoff with jitter, a limit on concurrency, and respect for retry-after; consider batching or a queue. Slow down instead of hammering. Incident: The key in the screenshot Incident A teammate shared a terminal screenshot in a public channel that included an API key. $ env | grep -i anthropic ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX $ curl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code}\\n\" https://api.example.com/v1/models -H \"x-api-key: $OLD_KEY \" 401 The key was revoked as soon as the message was noticed, which is why the check now returns 401. A new key went into the secret manager, usage logs were reviewed for unexpected requests, and the team stopped printing environment variables in shared screens and added secret scanning to chat and repositories. Common mistakes Hard-coding keys Retrying client errors No timeout on API calls Logging full prompts that contain personal data"},"llm/cost-latency-and-caching":{"html":"\u003cp>Model calls are priced per token and take noticeable time. A feature that is cheap in a demo can be expensive at ten thousand users a day, and slow enough to frustrate people. A little arithmetic before launch, and a few well-known techniques, keep both under control.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Estimate cost from tokens and traffic\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Time to first token and tokens per second\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cut cost: smaller models, shorter prompts, caching, batching\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Budgets and alerts\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"back-of-envelope-cost\">Back-of-envelope cost\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cost per request = input tokens times the input price, plus output tokens times the output price. Output tokens usually cost several times more than input tokens. Example with made-up prices: 3,000 input tokens at 3 dollars per million is 0.009 dollars, 500 output tokens at 15 dollars per million is 0.0075 dollars, so about 1.65 cents per request. At 50,000 requests per day that is about 825 dollars a day. Use your provider&#39;s current price list, and measure your real token counts.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"def cost(inp, out, price_in=3.0, price_out=15.0):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Prices are dollars per million tokens (example values).&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    return inp / 1e6 * price_in + out / 1e6 * price_out\n\nprint(round(cost(3000, 500) * 50_000, 2))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">cost\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">inp, out, price_in=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3.0\u003c/span>, price_out=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">15.0\u003c/span>\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;&quot;Prices are dollars per million tokens (example values).&quot;&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> inp / \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1e6\u003c/span> * price_in + out / \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1e6\u003c/span> * price_out\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">round\u003c/span>(cost(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3000\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">500\u003c/span>) * \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">50_000\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"latency\">Latency\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Total time is roughly: network and queueing, \u003cstrong>time to first token\u003c/strong> (processing your input), and then output length divided by \u003cstrong>tokens per second\u003c/strong>. A long prompt delays the first token; a long answer takes long to finish. Streaming improves perceived speed. Smaller models are faster and cheaper, so use the smallest model that passes your tests for each step.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reducing-cost-and-delay\">Reducing cost and delay\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Right-size the model\u003c/strong>: easy steps (classify, extract) on a small model, hard steps on a large one.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Shorten\u003c/strong>: trim instructions, remove unused context, ask for concise output or a fixed format.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Prompt caching\u003c/strong>: mark a large stable prefix (instructions, reference text, tool definitions) so repeated requests reuse it at lower cost and delay. Put stable content first and changing content last.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Batch\u003c/strong> non-urgent work through a batch interface, often at a discount.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Cache answers\u003c/strong> to identical questions in your own store, if the content allows.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Limit\u003c/strong> output with max_tokens and stop conditions.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"budgets-and-visibility\">Budgets and visibility\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Track tokens and cost per feature, per customer and per model. Set spend limits at the provider and alerts in your monitoring. Add a circuit breaker for runaway loops, especially in agents that call a model repeatedly. One forgotten retry loop can spend a month&#39;s budget overnight.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A feature calls the large model three times per request to classify, extract and summarise. How can you cut cost without losing quality?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Use a small model for classification and extraction, cache the stable prefix, and keep the large model for the summary only; confirm with your tests.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Match model size to the step.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-overnight-bill\">Incident: The overnight bill\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A scheduled job re-sent the same 40,000-token document 2,000 times.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python usage_report.py --day yesterday\npython usage_report.py --day yesterday --cached\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python usage_report.py --day yesterday\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">feature=doc-summary requests=2000 input_tokens=80,412,000 output_tokens=610,000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python usage_report.py --day yesterday --cached\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">cache_read_tokens=0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Nothing was cached, and the document was the same in every request. The team moved the document into a cached prefix, used a batch process for the nightly run, and added a daily spend alert and a hard stop at a set budget.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No token or cost tracking\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using the largest model for every step\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting changing text before the stable prefix\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No budget limit on automated loops\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"back-of-envelope-cost","text":"Back-of-envelope cost"},{"id":"latency","text":"Latency"},{"id":"reducing-cost-and-delay","text":"Reducing cost and delay"},{"id":"budgets-and-visibility","text":"Budgets and visibility"},{"id":"incident-the-overnight-bill","text":"Incident: The overnight bill"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Prompt caching","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/prompt-caching"},{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Message Batches","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/batch-processing"}],"text":"Model calls are priced per token and take noticeable time. A feature that is cheap in a demo can be expensive at ten thousand users a day, and slow enough to frustrate people. A little arithmetic before launch, and a few well-known techniques, keep both under control. What you will learn Estimate cost from tokens and traffic Time to first token and tokens per second Cut cost: smaller models, shorter prompts, caching, batching Budgets and alerts Back-of-envelope cost Cost per request = input tokens times the input price, plus output tokens times the output price. Output tokens usually cost several times more than input tokens. Example with made-up prices: 3,000 input tokens at 3 dollars per million is 0.009 dollars, 500 output tokens at 15 dollars per million is 0.0075 dollars, so about 1.65 cents per request. At 50,000 requests per day that is about 825 dollars a day. Use your provider&#39;s current price list, and measure your real token counts. def cost ( inp, out, price_in= 3.0 , price_out= 15.0 ): \"\"\"Prices are dollars per million tokens (example values).\"\"\" return inp / 1e6 * price_in + out / 1e6 * price_out print ( round (cost( 3000 , 500 ) * 50_000 , 2 )) Latency Total time is roughly: network and queueing, time to first token (processing your input), and then output length divided by tokens per second . A long prompt delays the first token; a long answer takes long to finish. Streaming improves perceived speed. Smaller models are faster and cheaper, so use the smallest model that passes your tests for each step. Reducing cost and delay Right-size the model : easy steps (classify, extract) on a small model, hard steps on a large one. Shorten : trim instructions, remove unused context, ask for concise output or a fixed format. Prompt caching : mark a large stable prefix (instructions, reference text, tool definitions) so repeated requests reuse it at lower cost and delay. Put stable content first and changing content last. Batch non-urgent work through a batch interface, often at a discount. Cache answers to identical questions in your own store, if the content allows. Limit output with max_tokens and stop conditions. Budgets and visibility Track tokens and cost per feature, per customer and per model. Set spend limits at the provider and alerts in your monitoring. Add a circuit breaker for runaway loops, especially in agents that call a model repeatedly. One forgotten retry loop can spend a month&#39;s budget overnight. Predict first. A feature calls the large model three times per request to classify, extract and summarise. How can you cut cost without losing quality? Your guess Answer: Use a small model for classification and extraction, cache the stable prefix, and keep the large model for the summary only; confirm with your tests. Match model size to the step. Incident: The overnight bill Incident A scheduled job re-sent the same 40,000-token document 2,000 times. $ python usage_report.py --day yesterday feature=doc-summary requests=2000 input_tokens=80,412,000 output_tokens=610,000 $ python usage_report.py --day yesterday --cached cache_read_tokens=0 Nothing was cached, and the document was the same in every request. The team moved the document into a cached prefix, used a batch process for the nightly run, and added a daily spend alert and a hard stop at a set budget. Common mistakes No token or cost tracking Using the largest model for every step Putting changing text before the stable prefix No budget limit on automated loops"},"llm/choosing-and-evaluating-models":{"html":"\u003cp>Public benchmarks measure general ability. Your feature needs one specific thing done well on your data. The only reliable way to choose a model or a prompt is to test it on examples that look like your real traffic, and to keep those tests running as things change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why you need your own evaluation set\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Build a set of examples with expected properties\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Score outputs: exact checks, rules and model-based grading\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Compare, decide and keep testing\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-your-own-tests\">Why your own tests\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Benchmarks can be outdated, narrow, or leaked into training data, and they use tasks unlike yours. A model that ranks first on a leaderboard may do worse on your support tickets or your log format. A small set of 30 to 100 real, varied examples with known good answers will tell you more about your feature than any public score.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"build-the-set\">Build the set\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Collect real inputs (with personal data removed), include normal cases, hard cases, and cases where the right answer is &quot;I don&#39;t know&quot; or &quot;escalate&quot;. For each, write what a good answer must contain or must not contain. Keep a held-out part you do not tune on. Store the set in version control next to the prompt.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"{&quot;id&quot;: &quot;t-014&quot;, &quot;input&quot;: &quot;Customer says card was charged twice&quot;, &quot;expect&quot;: {&quot;category&quot;: &quot;billing&quot;, &quot;must_not_say&quot;: [&quot;refund approved&quot;]}}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;id&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;t-014&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;input&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Customer says card was charged twice&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;expect&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;category&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;billing&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;must_not_say&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;refund approved&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"score-the-answers\">Score the answers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Exact checks\u003c/strong> for structured fields: category in the allowed list, number in range, valid JSON.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Rules\u003c/strong> for text: contains required facts, avoids forbidden claims, stays under a length.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Model-based grading\u003c/strong>: another model compares the answer to a rubric. Fast and useful, but it makes mistakes and has biases, so spot-check it with humans and prefer clear rubrics with yes or no questions.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Human review\u003c/strong> of a sample, especially for tone and safety.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"def score(case, output):\n    ok = output.get(&quot;category&quot;) in ALLOWED\n    ok = ok and output.get(&quot;category&quot;) == case[&quot;expect&quot;][&quot;category&quot;]\n    ok = ok and not any(w in output.get(&quot;reply&quot;, &quot;&quot;) for w in case[&quot;expect&quot;].get(&quot;must_not_say&quot;, []))\n    return ok\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">score\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">case\u003c/span>, output\u003c/span>):\n    ok = output.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;category&quot;\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> ALLOWED\n    ok = ok \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">and\u003c/span> output.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;category&quot;\u003c/span>) == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">case\u003c/span>[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;expect&quot;\u003c/span>][\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;category&quot;\u003c/span>]\n    ok = ok \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">and\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">any\u003c/span>(w \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> output.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;reply&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> w \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">case\u003c/span>[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;expect&quot;\u003c/span>].get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;must_not_say&quot;\u003c/span>, []))\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> ok\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"decide-and-keep-testing\">Decide and keep testing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run each candidate model and prompt on the same set, several times, and compare pass rate, cost and latency. Choose the cheapest option that meets your quality bar. Re-run the set when you change the prompt, the model version, the retrieval, or the tools. Add every production failure as a new test case. This is a regression test for behaviour that cannot be unit tested in the old way.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A new model version scores higher on a public benchmark. Should you switch production to it today?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Not before running your own evaluation set; check quality, cost and latency on your data, then roll out gradually.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Your data decides.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-upgrade-that-broke-the-router\">Incident: The upgrade that broke the router\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A model upgrade changed how the ticket router phrased its categories.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python run_evals.py --model new --set tickets\npython run_evals.py --model old --set tickets\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python run_evals.py --model new --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> tickets\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">pass_rate=0.71 (was 0.94)   failures: category_not_in_allowed_list=38\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python run_evals.py --model old --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> tickets\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">pass_rate=0.94\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The new version added explanations to the category field, so parsing failed. Because the team had an evaluation set in CI, the problem showed up before release. They tightened the prompt and the schema, re-ran the set until the pass rate was back, and then switched gradually.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choosing a model by leaderboard alone\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tuning on the same examples you evaluate on\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using only model-based grading with no human check\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not re-running tests after changes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"why-your-own-tests","text":"Why your own tests"},{"id":"build-the-set","text":"Build the set"},{"id":"score-the-answers","text":"Score the answers"},{"id":"decide-and-keep-testing","text":"Decide and keep testing"},{"id":"incident-the-upgrade-that-broke-the-router","text":"Incident: The upgrade that broke the router"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Define success criteria","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/test-and-evaluate/define-success"},{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Develop test cases","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests"}],"text":"Public benchmarks measure general ability. Your feature needs one specific thing done well on your data. The only reliable way to choose a model or a prompt is to test it on examples that look like your real traffic, and to keep those tests running as things change. What you will learn Why you need your own evaluation set Build a set of examples with expected properties Score outputs: exact checks, rules and model-based grading Compare, decide and keep testing Why your own tests Benchmarks can be outdated, narrow, or leaked into training data, and they use tasks unlike yours. A model that ranks first on a leaderboard may do worse on your support tickets or your log format. A small set of 30 to 100 real, varied examples with known good answers will tell you more about your feature than any public score. Build the set Collect real inputs (with personal data removed), include normal cases, hard cases, and cases where the right answer is \"I don&#39;t know\" or \"escalate\". For each, write what a good answer must contain or must not contain. Keep a held-out part you do not tune on. Store the set in version control next to the prompt. { \"id\" : \"t-014\" , \"input\" : \"Customer says card was charged twice\" , \"expect\" : { \"category\" : \"billing\" , \"must_not_say\" : [ \"refund approved\" ] } } Score the answers Exact checks for structured fields: category in the allowed list, number in range, valid JSON. Rules for text: contains required facts, avoids forbidden claims, stays under a length. Model-based grading : another model compares the answer to a rubric. Fast and useful, but it makes mistakes and has biases, so spot-check it with humans and prefer clear rubrics with yes or no questions. Human review of a sample, especially for tone and safety. def score ( case , output ): ok = output.get( \"category\" ) in ALLOWED ok = ok and output.get( \"category\" ) == case [ \"expect\" ][ \"category\" ] ok = ok and not any (w in output.get( \"reply\" , \"\" ) for w in case [ \"expect\" ].get( \"must_not_say\" , [])) return ok Decide and keep testing Run each candidate model and prompt on the same set, several times, and compare pass rate, cost and latency. Choose the cheapest option that meets your quality bar. Re-run the set when you change the prompt, the model version, the retrieval, or the tools. Add every production failure as a new test case. This is a regression test for behaviour that cannot be unit tested in the old way. Predict first. A new model version scores higher on a public benchmark. Should you switch production to it today? Your guess Answer: Not before running your own evaluation set; check quality, cost and latency on your data, then roll out gradually. Your data decides. Incident: The upgrade that broke the router Incident A model upgrade changed how the ticket router phrased its categories. $ python run_evals.py --model new -- set tickets pass_rate=0.71 (was 0.94) failures: category_not_in_allowed_list=38 $ python run_evals.py --model old -- set tickets pass_rate=0.94 The new version added explanations to the category field, so parsing failed. Because the team had an evaluation set in CI, the problem showed up before release. They tightened the prompt and the schema, re-ran the set until the pass rate was back, and then switched gradually. Common mistakes Choosing a model by leaderboard alone Tuning on the same examples you evaluate on Using only model-based grading with no human check Not re-running tests after changes"},"llm/debugging-llm-applications":{"html":"\u003cp>When an LLM feature misbehaves there is no stack trace. The system is probabilistic and the failure may be in retrieval, the prompt, the model, the limits or the code around it. A fixed order of checks finds the cause quickly and turns each incident into a permanent test.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Capture the exact request and response\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Check limits, stop reasons and errors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Separate retrieval, prompt, model and parsing problems\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reproduce, fix and add a regression test\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"capture-everything\">Capture everything\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>You cannot debug what you cannot see. For every request, log the request ID, model and version, the full assembled prompt (with personal data protected), parameters, retrieved passages, tool calls and results, the raw reply, the stop reason, token counts and latency. Store them for a limited time with access controls. A trace that connects one user report to one stored request is worth more than any guess.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"first-checks\">First checks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"jq '{id, model, stop_reason, usage, error}' logs/llm/2026-10-28/req-8f31.json\njq -r '.prompt' logs/llm/2026-10-28/req-8f31.json | head -60\njq -r '.reply' logs/llm/2026-10-28/req-8f31.json\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">jq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{id, model, stop_reason, usage, error}&#x27;\u003c/span> logs/llm/2026-10-28/req-8f31.json\njq -r \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.prompt&#x27;\u003c/span> logs/llm/2026-10-28/req-8f31.json | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -60\njq -r \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.reply&#x27;\u003c/span> logs/llm/2026-10-28/req-8f31.json\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Look for: an error or a \u003cstrong>429/529\u003c/strong>; \u003cstrong>stop_reason\u003c/strong> other than a normal end (cut off by length); the \u003cstrong>model version\u003c/strong> changed; the prompt contains stale or missing text; a very large input that pushed out instructions; the reply that is valid text but fails your parser.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"which-layer-failed\">Which layer failed?\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Retrieval or context\u003c/strong>: is the needed fact in the prompt? If not, fix search, chunking or the data. The model cannot use what it never saw.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Prompt\u003c/strong>: is the instruction clear, are examples consistent, do two rules conflict? Try the same input with a simplified prompt.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Model\u003c/strong>: with the right facts and a clear prompt, does it still fail? Try a stronger model or break the task into steps.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Parsing and code\u003c/strong>: is the output valid but your code mishandles it, for example by assuming the first content block is text?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Environment\u003c/strong>: did a tool fail silently, or return an error that the model had to guess around?\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fix-and-lock-it-in\">Fix and lock it in\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Reproduce with the stored request, change one thing, and re-run. Then add the case to the evaluation set with the expected behaviour, so it cannot return unnoticed. Compare the new pass rate with the old on the whole set, not only on the case you fixed. Record the cause in your runbook: most incidents repeat. Add monitors for the signals that would have caught this earlier, such as parse-failure rate, empty-answer rate, refusal rate, token growth and user thumbs-down rate.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A support bot suddenly says &quot;I don't have that information&quot; for questions it answered yesterday. Which layer do you check first?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Retrieval and context: check whether the relevant document is still being found and included, before blaming the model.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Is the fact in the prompt?\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-bot-that-stopped-finding-the-policy\">Incident: The bot that stopped finding the policy\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a documentation update, a support assistant began refusing policy questions.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"jq '{retrieved: [.retrieved[].source], stop_reason}' req-1204.json\njq '.retrieved[].source' req-0711-last-week.json\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>jq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{retrieved: [.retrieved[].source], stop_reason}&#x27;\u003c/span> req-1204.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{ &quot;retrieved&quot;: [&quot;blog/holiday-hours.md&quot;, &quot;faq/shipping.md&quot;], &quot;stop_reason&quot;: &quot;end_turn&quot; }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>jq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.retrieved[].source&#x27;\u003c/span> req-0711-last-week.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&quot;policies/returns-v3.md&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The reindexing job had skipped the policies folder after a path change, so the returns policy was never retrieved and the model correctly said it did not know. The index was rebuilt, a monitor was added for documents that are not found for known test questions, and those questions became part of the nightly evaluation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Blaming the model before checking the prompt contents\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not logging the assembled prompt\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fixing one case without a regression test\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changing several things at once\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"capture-everything","text":"Capture everything"},{"id":"first-checks","text":"First checks"},{"id":"which-layer-failed","text":"Which layer failed?"},{"id":"fix-and-lock-it-in","text":"Fix and lock it in"},{"id":"incident-the-bot-that-stopped-finding-the-policy","text":"Incident: The bot that stopped finding the policy"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Reduce latency","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-latency"},{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Errors","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/api/errors"}],"text":"When an LLM feature misbehaves there is no stack trace. The system is probabilistic and the failure may be in retrieval, the prompt, the model, the limits or the code around it. A fixed order of checks finds the cause quickly and turns each incident into a permanent test. What you will learn Capture the exact request and response Check limits, stop reasons and errors Separate retrieval, prompt, model and parsing problems Reproduce, fix and add a regression test Capture everything You cannot debug what you cannot see. For every request, log the request ID, model and version, the full assembled prompt (with personal data protected), parameters, retrieved passages, tool calls and results, the raw reply, the stop reason, token counts and latency. Store them for a limited time with access controls. A trace that connects one user report to one stored request is worth more than any guess. First checks jq &#x27;{id, model, stop_reason, usage, error}&#x27; logs/llm/2026-10-28/req-8f31.json jq -r &#x27;.prompt&#x27; logs/llm/2026-10-28/req-8f31.json | head -60 jq -r &#x27;.reply&#x27; logs/llm/2026-10-28/req-8f31.json Look for: an error or a 429/529 ; stop_reason other than a normal end (cut off by length); the model version changed; the prompt contains stale or missing text; a very large input that pushed out instructions; the reply that is valid text but fails your parser. Which layer failed? Retrieval or context : is the needed fact in the prompt? If not, fix search, chunking or the data. The model cannot use what it never saw. Prompt : is the instruction clear, are examples consistent, do two rules conflict? Try the same input with a simplified prompt. Model : with the right facts and a clear prompt, does it still fail? Try a stronger model or break the task into steps. Parsing and code : is the output valid but your code mishandles it, for example by assuming the first content block is text? Environment : did a tool fail silently, or return an error that the model had to guess around? Fix and lock it in Reproduce with the stored request, change one thing, and re-run. Then add the case to the evaluation set with the expected behaviour, so it cannot return unnoticed. Compare the new pass rate with the old on the whole set, not only on the case you fixed. Record the cause in your runbook: most incidents repeat. Add monitors for the signals that would have caught this earlier, such as parse-failure rate, empty-answer rate, refusal rate, token growth and user thumbs-down rate. Predict first. A support bot suddenly says \"I don't have that information\" for questions it answered yesterday. Which layer do you check first? Your guess Answer: Retrieval and context: check whether the relevant document is still being found and included, before blaming the model. Is the fact in the prompt? Incident: The bot that stopped finding the policy Incident After a documentation update, a support assistant began refusing policy questions. $ jq &#x27;{retrieved: [.retrieved[].source], stop_reason}&#x27; req-1204.json { \"retrieved\": [\"blog/holiday-hours.md\", \"faq/shipping.md\"], \"stop_reason\": \"end_turn\" } $ jq &#x27;.retrieved[].source&#x27; req-0711-last-week.json \"policies/returns-v3.md\" The reindexing job had skipped the policies folder after a path change, so the returns policy was never retrieved and the model correctly said it did not know. The index was rebuilt, a monitor was added for documents that are not found for known test questions, and those questions became part of the nightly evaluation. Common mistakes Blaming the model before checking the prompt contents Not logging the assembled prompt Fixing one case without a regression test Changing several things at once"}});